WasherZones draws one package feeding a cabinet washer as an isometric line drawing with four dashed, numbered zones keyed to four rows of systems, each row carrying its standard as a navy chip.

- On the site: a `grid` block with `"look": "washer-zones"` (renderer `assets/src/blocks/look-washer-zones.js`); ind-meddevice.4 "Systems for device plants" (#systems). Origin: pass 12 concept `design-system/inspiration/gemini/227-p12-ind-meddevice-systems-take2.png` (idea only: its two zone frames carrying all four numbers, the painted tank name, the wordmark and its labels are not ours; the builder draws four zones round the right parts).
- Fields, as grid: `h`, `intro`, `items` [{`t`, `d`, `icon`, `tag`, `link`}], four items. No look-only fields: the drawing is fixed, the zones are chosen by `icon`: `ro-membrane` → generation (the RO rack and its run to the tank), `loop` → storage and loop (the tank; the loop's supply and return go with it), `conductivity` → monitoring (analyser sampling the return, control panel), `valve` → retrofit tie-in (a valve on a short level leg of the drop, run forward under the return so its valve bonnet sits clear of the header, and the washer). An unknown icon takes the next free zone in that order.
- The drawing (IsoKit, light sheet): the RO rack, a pipe up and over into the tank, the loop out of the tank, up a riser and along a high header to the washer, a hairpin and the return home past the analyser into the tank. The washer is drawn here from primitives (the kit has no washer part): cabinet, control strip with a display, door with its window, louvres on the side. The tie-in valve is the kit's default valve (no named valve type, DR-018). No flanges, no names or figures on the equipment.
- Markup: `div.aq-washer-zones[data-snap-box][data-cams]` (`--n` items) → `div.__fig` (aria-hidden) > `div.__cam` (`--ar` the drawing's aspect) > `svg.aq-iso.__svg` (zone groups `g.__z[data-z]`, the loop pipes `g.__z--loop`, frames `g.__frames` > `rect.__frame[data-z]`) and `span.__tag[data-z][data-k]` (HTML, at each frame's top right corner, inside; zone 2's sits on its bottom left corner, `.__tag--bl`, clear of the riser's bend into the header); `ol.__rows[data-snap]` of `li.__row[data-z][data-k]` > `a.__in` (`span.__no`, `span.__chip` the tag, `h3.__t`, `span.__d`, `span.__arrow`); `div.__dots` of `button.__dot[data-snap-to]` (phones). `data-cams` holds each zone's centre and size as fractions of the drawing, for the phone camera.
- Desktop: the drawing centred (up to 460 px tall), the four rows as outlined cards in a row under it (two by two at 1,100 px and under). Pointing at or focusing a row lights its zone: the frame and tag turn `flow`, the zone's equipment is drawn in `flow`, the other zones fall to 30 % opacity, the row gets a 3 px `flow` top rule. Leaving the rows clears it.
- **Phones** (≤ 860 px container): the drawing becomes a strip between hairlines; the rows a swipe rail of cards (84 %, max 340 px, the next one peeking, scroll snap), one zone per card. The card in view (AQsnap's `.is-snap`) is the zone the strip frames: the drawing pans and zooms (1 to 2.2 ×) so that zone sits centred (a strip 600 px wide or more, as on a tablet, keeps the whole drawing at 1 × and only lights the zone), lit as on desktop; tags keep their 12 px size at any zoom. Numbered dots under the rail (44 px targets, mono 12, the current one navy) jump between cards. Cards: number 28 px, title 18/24, line 15/22, the whole card is the link. Without script every zone shows and the rail still swipes. Section height at 375: 614 px (StandardTiles there: 1,418).
- Tokens: `line-strong` frames and card rules on `surface`, `navy` chips and tags with `on-navy`, `flow` for the lit zone, `ink` / `ink-muted` text. Dark theme follows the tokens.
- Not for more than four items or items that are not parts of one plant; use StandardTiles or a grid look for those.

## Motion

`motion.js`: the package draws itself the IsoKit way (`AQiso.timeline`: equipment back to front, pipes, then the water runs the route once with a droplet), and each zone stamps in as the water reaches it (frame settles in from 96 %, tag wipes down onto its corner): generation as the water sets off, then storage and loop, the washer's tie-in, and monitoring on the return. The rows rise in under the drawing. Ported from Subframe Motion "Animated workflow diagram" (process-choreography), MIT, (c) 2026 Subframe. Once on scroll-in, under 4 s.

The phone camera is ported from Subframe Motion "Guided inspection" (guided-inspection), MIT, (c) 2026 Subframe: one transform pans and zooms the drawing to the stop, 550 ms `cubic-bezier(.22,.75,.22,1)` in CSS. It and the desktop zone focus live in the renderer (state, not motion), so they work under reduced motion, where the camera jumps and the reveal does not run (the finished drawing).
